        "id": "ch02.open.p01",
        "sentences": [
          {
            "id": "ch02.open.p01.01",
            "text": "The previous chapter established what large language models are and where transformers fit; this chapter turns to the practical question of how raw text becomes something a model can actually consume.",
            "type": "R",
            "cites": []
          },
          {
            "id": "ch02.open.p01.02",
            "text": "Data preprocessing matters because it is presented as one of the most fundamental building blocks of a strongly performing large language model, and because text such as paragraphs, documents, or a PDF file cannot be given directly as input and must be processed first.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "mk-6cFebjis@13",
              "mk-6cFebjis@15",
              "mk-6cFebjis@18",
              "mk-6cFebjis@20",
              "mk-6cFebjis@30",
              "mk-6cFebjis@32",
              "mk-6cFebjis@36",
              "mk-6cFebjis@50",
              "mk-6cFebjis@54",
              "mk-6cFebjis@56",
              "mk-6cFebjis@59",
              "mk-6cFebjis@63",
              "mk-6cFebjis@66",
              "mk-6cFebjis@70",
              "mk-6cFebjis@72"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch02.open.p01.03",
            "text": "The core pipeline runs from tokenization to token IDs to token embeddings to positional embeddings and finally to input embeddings, which makes the data ready to be fed into the model architecture or pre-training process.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "mk-6cFebjis@92",
              "mk-6cFebjis@94",
              "mk-6cFebjis@98",
