            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "ch02.open.p02",
        "sentences": [
          {
            "id": "ch02.open.p02.01",
            "text": "We begin with why preprocessing exists, then build intuition with word-based tokenization, a simple Python tokenizer, and the limits of that approach, including unknown and boundary tokens.",
            "type": "R",
            "cites": []
          },
          {
            "id": "ch02.open.p02.02",
            "text": "From there the chapter broadens to character and subword tokenization, introduces byte pair encoding and its role in modern LLM tokenizers, and uses the GPT-2 tokenizer through \"tiktoken\" while noting that the mechanics of subword BPE are only introduced indirectly here.",
            "type": "R",
            "cites": []
          },
          {
            "id": "ch02.open.p02.03",
            "text": "The closing sections move from token IDs to token embeddings, embedding matrices and lookup layers, positional information, and the creation of next-token training examples, setting up the attention machinery of the next chapter.",
            "type": "R",
            "cites": []
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "ch02.glance.p01",
        "sentences": [
          {
            "id": "ch02.glance.p01.01",
            "text": "Preprocessing is the procedure of processing text data before it is fed into a large language model, and it is described as a fundamental building block for strong model performance.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "mk-6cFebjis@30",
              "mk-6cFebjis@32",
              "mk-6cFebjis@36",
              "mk-6cFebjis@63",
              "mk-6cFebjis@66",
              "mk-6cFebjis@70",
              "mk-6cFebjis@72"
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "ch02.glance.p02",
        "sentences": [
          {
            "id": "ch02.glance.p02.01",
            "text": "A model input pipeline starts by breaking input text into tokens, converting those tokens into token IDs, and then converting the token IDs into token embeddings used to train the model.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@58",
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@60",
